/* logtriage — the corpus's first program written with PATTERNS.

   A log is the honest case for them. Nobody writes a log for the thing that
   reads it: the format drifts, a stack trace lands in the middle without a
   timestamp, and half the interesting lines are interesting because of a shape
   rather than a word. `contains` answers "is this exact text in there"; a
   pattern answers "is this SHAPE in there", and that is the whole difference.

       cufet examples/parsing/logtriage.cufe
       cufet examples/parsing/logtriage.cufe /var/log/app.log

   The exit contract, so a shell can branch on it:

       0   the log is clean — nothing serious in it
       1   the log holds at least one ERROR or FATAL line
       2   the file could not be read

   ⚠ That 1 means something DIFFERENT from search's 1, and deliberately so. In
   `search` it means "nothing matched" — an answer. Here it means "something
   matched, and you should care". Both are "the thing you asked about", which is
   the only contract an exit status can honestly carry: a status has no room for
   a sentence, so it must answer the question the program is named for.

   ── What patterns can and cannot do here, measured while writing this ──

   ★★ A pattern asks whether a subject HOLDS a match, never whether the subject
   IS one. That is not a gap in this program, it is the shape of the tool: every
   question below is genuinely "does this line contain such a thing".

   ★ It WAS a gap in the book, though, and this program is what found it: with no
   way to say "and that is the whole line", validation had no spelling at all —
   `[[0-9]+]` is held by "abc 42" just as firmly as by "42". The limit arrived as
   a refusal naming `^` rather than as a wrong answer, and anchors landed in the
   release after this program was written. They are `^` and `$` now, and a line
   like `[^[0-9]+$]` says what could not be said when this was first written.

   ⚠ A pattern also cannot be built at run time, so this program cannot take one
   on its command line the way grep does. That is the same guarantee that makes
   injection impossible here, and it is why the shapes below are written in the
   source where a reader can check them.

   ★ And a pattern gives back a `fact`, not the text it matched — there are no
   captures. So this counts and flags; it cannot pull the timestamp OUT of a
   line. Every question it asks is therefore a yes-or-no one, which is why the
   report is counts rather than fields.

   Exercises: alternation, groups, `+`, character classes, a class ending in a
   dash, and an escaped `\.` — the dot that means "any character" everywhere
   else.
*/

Pull a book on regex.
    /* ERROR and FATAL are one question, not two: a caller branching on the exit
       status wants "is something broken", and the two words are one answer. */
    Define regex serious as [(ERROR|FATAL)].

    Define regex caution as [WARN].

    /* `2026-09-11 08:14:02` — the shape, not the values. Written loosely on
       purpose: `[0-9]+` rather than four-then-two-then-two, because without
       anchors a stricter pattern buys nothing a looser one does not already
       get, and reads as though it were checking something it is not. */
    Define regex stamped as [[0-9]+-[0-9]+-[0-9]+ [0-9]+:[0-9]+:[0-9]+].

    /* ⚠ The dots are ESCAPED. Unescaped, `.` is "any character", and the pattern
       would then be held by `10x0y0z14` — and by the timestamps, which is how a
       silent wrong answer would have arrived. */
    Define regex address as [[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+].

    /* ★ `[a-z-]` ends in a dash, which is an ordinary character there rather
       than a half-written range — that rule is what lets `/api/v3/orders-beta`
       match without spelling the dash some other way. */
    Define regex route as [/api/v[0-9]+/[a-z-]+].

    Bind void to usage:
        State "usage: logtriage [file]".
        State "".
        State "reads a log and reports what shapes its lines hold".
        State "leaves with 0 if nothing is serious, 1 if something is, 2 on a bad file".
    Done.

    Bind void to play:
        Define target as "examples/assets/server.log".

        If the number of the arguments is greater than 1:
            Cast usage.
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        If the number of the arguments is 0:
            State "(no arguments — reading the log that ships with this example)".
            State "".
        Done.
        Otherwise:
            The target becomes item 1 of the arguments.
        Done.

        Define lines as a series of text.
        Try to:
            The lines becomes read all lines from the file target.
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            State "logtriage: {target}: {the message of the failure}".
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define flagged as 0.
        Define warnings as 0.
        Define undated as 0.
        Define addressed as 0.
        Define routed as 0.
        Define index as 0.

        For each subject in lines, repeat:
            Increment index by 1.

            /* Printed as it is found rather than collected and printed after, so
               the order of the report is the order of the log. A log read out of
               order is a different log. */
            If cast serious on (subject):
                Increment flagged by 1.
                State "{target}:{index converted to text}: {subject}".
            Done.

            If cast caution on (subject), increment warnings by 1.
            If cast address on (subject), increment addressed by 1.
            If cast route on (subject), increment routed by 1.

            /* The only question asked in the negative, and the useful one: a line
               with no timestamp is a continuation of the line above it, or it is
               damage. Either way it is not a record. */
            If cast stamped on (subject) is false, increment undated by 1.
        Done.

        If flagged is greater than 0, state "".

        State "{the number of lines converted to text} line(s) read from {target}".
        State "  {flagged converted to text} serious (ERROR or FATAL)".
        State "  {warnings converted to text} warning(s)".
        State "  {undated converted to text} line(s) carrying no timestamp".
        State "  {addressed converted to text} line(s) naming an address".
        State "  {routed converted to text} line(s) naming an api route".

        If flagged is greater than 0, exit with 1.
        Exit.
    Done.

    Cast play.
Done.
